[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1m1. Off-by-one — fixed.[0m [38;5;153msrc/ratelimit/limiter.py:23[39m now uses [38;5;153mlen(self._events) < self.limit[39m. With [38;5;153mlimit=3[39m the old
  comparison admitted a 4th event (empty deque passed [38;5;153m0 <= 3[39m, and so on through [38;5;153m3 <= 3[39m).
  [38;5;153mtests/test_limiter.py::test_does_not_exceed_limit[39m was already failing on this ([38;5;153massert 4 == 3[39m); both tests pass now.

  [1m2. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m — pushing back.[0m The monotonic clock is a deliberate choice, documented in the class docstring at
  [38;5;153mlimiter.py:8-10[39m. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m is not monotonic: an NTP step backwards makes [38;5;153mcutoff[39m recede so old events never expire
  (the limiter stalls and rejects legitimate traffic); a step forwards expires the whole window at once and lets a full
  burst through. Those are exactly the failure modes the docstring cites, and they're live concerns for a gateway
  component.

  The underlying need — correlating with wall-clock log timestamps — doesn't require the window arithmetic to use
  wall-clock. If that correlation is the real goal, I'd store both per event (monotonic for expiry, wall-clock for
  emission) or just emit [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at log time. Want me to add the wall-clock field for logging? Swapping the window
  clock itself trades a correctness property for a logging convenience.

  [1m3. Storage-backend interface — pushing back on YAGNI grounds.[0m Nothing in this repo references Redis or any second
  backend; the only consumer surface is [38;5;153mSlidingWindowLimiter.allow()[39m. An abstract backend today means designing an
  interface against a hypothetical Redis implementation, and a sliding window over Redis is not a deque behind a
  different method — it's [38;5;153mZADD[39m/[38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m in a Lua script for atomicity, with the clock living server-side. An
  interface guessed at now would almost certainly be the wrong shape and get rewritten anyway.

  Concretely: is Redis actually planned? If it's on the roadmap I'd rather write the Redis limiter first and extract the
  interface from two real implementations. If it isn't, I'd leave the deque alone.

  Item 1 is committed to the working tree; 2 and 3 are unchanged pending your call.

